  <h1>Best dog food for itchy skin and allergies</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">If your dog scratches constantly and there are no fleas, the food bowl is the first place to look. Most itchy-skin cases trace to one of three causes — and one of them you can fix this month without a prescription.</p>

  <h2>Why is my dog so itchy?</h2>
  <p>Three culprits cover most itching: parasites (fleas, mites), environmental allergies (pollen, dust mites, grasses), and food sensitivities. Rule out fleas first — it takes one vet visit or a careful comb-through. If the itching is year-round rather than seasonal, and especially if it comes with recurring ear infections, paw licking, or gut trouble, food moves to the top of the suspect list.</p>

  <h2>What ingredients trigger itching most often?</h2>
  <p>Dogs are most commonly sensitive to proteins they've eaten for years — chicken and beef lead the list, with dairy, wheat, and egg behind them. It surprises people: it's rarely "grain" in the abstract, and it's rarely something exotic. It's the protein the cheap kibble has fed them every day since puppyhood.</p>

  <h2>What should I look for in a food?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A named, novel protein first.</strong> If chicken is suspect, look at salmon, lamb, or turkey — named on the panel, not "meat meal."</li>
    <li><strong>Omega-3 fatty acids.</strong> Fish oil ingredients (salmon oil, flaxseed) support the skin barrier directly. This is the best-evidenced supplement category for coat and skin.</li>
    <li><strong>A short, readable ingredient list.</strong> Every mystery ingredient is a variable you can't control during an elimination.</li>
    <li><strong>A formulation you can match to your dog.</strong> An 80-pound senior and an 8-pound terrier should not eat the same recipe. Personalized, vet-formulated plans exist now — use one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How long until I see a difference?</h2>
  <p>Give a genuine food change 4–6 weeks with nothing else changing — no new treats, no table scraps, no simultaneous new shampoo. Skin cells and coat turn over on that timescale. Keep a simple photo log; the before/after is usually unmistakable.</p>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem, not a food problem?</h2>
  <p>Open sores, hair loss in patches, head shaking, or a dog in obvious distress — see a vet before experimenting with diet. Food changes complement veterinary care; they don't replace it.</p>

